description:
Limit to the sum of all exposures to the persons in a special relationship with credit institution: 50% of own funds
According to the Credit Institutions Act (2008) persons in a special relationship with a credit institution to whom an exposure would be incurred or increased are:
1) the credit institution's shareholders owning 5 percent or more of shares with voting rights at the credit institution's general meeting;
2) the members of the credit institution's management and supervisory board and its procurators;
3) persons who have concluded employment contracts with the credit institution under preferential terms; and 
4) legal persons in which the credit institution holds a participation.

Exposure limit to a single person in a special relationship with credit institution after taking into account the effect of the credit risk mitigation: 10% of own funds
According to the Credit Institutions Act (2009) persons in a special relationship with a credit institution to whom an exposure would be incurred or increased are:
1) the credit institution's shareholders owning 5 percent or more of shares with voting rights at the credit institution's general meeting;
2) the members of the credit institution's management and supervisory board and its procurators;
3) persons who have concluded employment contracts with the credit institution under preferential terms; and 
4) legal persons in which the credit institution holds a participation.

The law of 16 March 2006 introduced into the law of 17 June 1992 major amendments in relation to the accounting regime of accounts published by credit institution:
i) transposition of the optional regime of the IAS Regulation (Article 5) allowing to extend the scope of application of the IAS standards to unlisted companies and to annual accounts;
ii) transposition of the Fair Value Directive (2001/65/EC) and the Accounts Modernisation Directive (2003/51/EC);
iii) introduction, among other things, of the option to use certain provisions of the IAS/IFRS standards ("IAS options").
